    trust_num: '03 · Verification', trust_title: 'Why “resolved” here means resolved',
    trust_sub: "No official or party worker can close a report by saying so. Neighbours on the spot confirm a fix. An admin can accept a cleanup only after checking the photos, and the report then says so openly. These rules run on the server — the website can't skip them.",
    trust_1: "Anyone reports a problem with a photo and location. It's public at once — illegal-activity reports are checked by a moderator first.",
    trust_2: "Whoever fixes it — officials included — claims the cleanup with a photo taken within {cr} m of the spot. That doesn't close the report.",
    trust_3: "{q} other people, each standing at the spot with their own new photo, must confirm it. The claimant can't confirm their own claim, and confirmations must come from more than one network.",
    trust_4: 'For {h} hours after that, anyone at the spot can dispute it. {dq} disputes throw the claim out and the report reopens. Repeat fake claimants are blocked.',
    trust_5: 'Every photo, confirmation and dispute stays on the public record. Moderators can remove abusive content, but nobody can mark a report fixed on their own.',

